//! External known-answer tests for `origin-crypto` (CLAUDE.md §59, owner decision 19 of
//! 2026-11-08): the NIST ACVP vector sets for SHA-2, SHA-3, SHAKE and ML-DSA signature
//! verification, and Wycheproof's Ed25519 vectors.
//!
//! The data in `tests/vectors/acvp` and `tests/vectors/wycheproof` is generated by
//! `tools/convert_kat_vectors.py` from the upstream files at pinned revisions and SHA-256 hashes
//! (`docs/engineering/SOURCE_PROVENANCE.md`). It is included only by this host-only test crate,
//! so it never reaches the shipping runtime (§5).
//!
//! Each module asserts the number of vectors it runs, so a vector cannot silently disappear, and
//! has negative controls: a corrupted vector, run through the same check, must fail.
//!
//! What does not run, and why (the counts are recorded in each data file's header):
//! - SHA-3 and SHAKE messages that are not a whole number of bytes. The crate takes `&[u8]`,
//!   because everything Origin OS hashes is bytes, so there is no bit-granular input to test.
//! - ML-DSA-44, HashML-DSA and the internal interface: the crate provides pure ML-DSA-65 and
//!   ML-DSA-87 only (§59), and the steps those vectors share with it are the ones run here.
//!
//! `ORIGIN_KAT_SCALE` is the largest ACVP large-data vector to run, in GiB. The default, 1, runs
//! the 1 GiB vector of each fixed-output hash, which is past the 2^32-bit message length where a
//! truncated length field would show. 8 runs all of them (15 GiB per hash), and 0 runs none.
#![forbid(unsafe_code)]

mod hashes;
mod mldsa;
mod support;
mod wycheproof;
